{
  "result": "The old library at the end of my street smells of dust and rain even on sunny days. Children push through its heavy doors after school, looking for dragons, lost keys, and answers to homework they will pretend to have forgotten by morning. The librarian knows every reader by name and by the book they carry home. She stamps cards with a thump that echoes down long aisles. I go there not only for books but for the quiet between shelves where time seems to sit still and listen.\n\nOn winter afternoons the windows fog and strangers become friends without speaking. Someone always leaves a pencil behind as if to mark a page in their own life. I take that pencil and write my name in the margin of a borrowed novel, hoping the next reader will know I was here. We borrow stories but we also lend moments to each other across years and distance. When I grow old I hope to remember the exact color of the afternoon light on those wooden tables. It fell on open pages like a hand turning them gently for me.\n\nThere is no need to be famous or loud to matter inside such a place. The world becomes smaller there, kinder, and full of second chances for everyone who walks in seeking something they cannot name yet. And when the doors close at dusk, the books keep whispering to the empty chairs until we return again tomorrow morning for more. Still hoping.",
